-## Configurable event denoising (November 2026)
-
-The C++ module optionally filters the camera event stream **before** updating
-both EROS (MoveEnet input) and SAE (optical flow). The original
-`/primi-ball-balance/eventCam/<camera>/raw:o` stream remains unchanged.
-
-Parameters in `config/moveEnetOFK.env`:
-
-| Setting | Initial value | Meaning |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| `NOISE_FILTER` | `true` | Enable/disable filtering, including for A/B comparisons. |
-| `NOISE_REFRACTORY_MS` | `1.0` | Reject same-polarity events at the same pixel within this interval. Set `0` to disable this stage. |
-| `NOISE_SPATIAL_MS` | `5.0` | Require an event at a **different neighboring pixel** within the preceding interval. Set `0` to disable this stage. |
-| `NOISE_RADIUS` | `1` | Neighbor radius in pixels; allowed range `1..3`. |
-
-The spatial stage excludes the center pixel to prevent an isolated hot pixel
-from supporting its own repeated events. Both stages update their temporal
-history even when events are rejected, allowing a neighboring event to
-subsequently provide spatial support. This is a **causal event filter**, not an
-image blur. It affects the inference input and optical-flow SAE, so joint
-tracking must be re-validated with and without filtering.
-
-At startup the C++ module logs the chosen settings. Every five seconds
-`[stats]` reports `raw_events`, `events_used`, `noise_refractory`,
-`noise_spatial`, and `invalid`; they are cumulative counts. The intended
-accounting is `raw_events = events_used + noise_refractory + noise_spatial`.
-An event outside the configured sensor bounds increments `invalid` and is
-excluded from `raw_events`.
-
-**Timing warning:** when the installed event-driven library was built without
-individual AE timestamps, the filter and SAE use packet-level timestamps.
-The spatial/refractory windows then have only packet-level precision; test
-tracking quality before using filtered joint outputs for robot control.
-
-To compare behavior, run once with `NOISE_FILTER=false`, then with
-`NOISE_FILTER=true`, keeping other settings unchanged. Stop the existing
-pipeline cleanly (Ctrl+C) before each restart. Do not restart the remote
-`zynqGrabber` solely to change these software filters.
+## Event filtering and input window (native event-driven APIs)
+
+The C++ acquisition input uses `ev::window<ev::AE>` from `event-driven/core.h`.
+A `readAll(true)` call consumes all newly arrived packets in a window, as in
+EDPR-APRIL. In contrast to the original EDPR-APRIL implementation, PRIMI uses
+`window::iterator::timestamp()` and `packetID()` to group consecutive events
+by their source packet, preserving packet-level timestamps instead of assigning
+the last window timestamp to every event. If consecutive packets have identical
+stamps they cannot be distinguished by this method. The upstream
+`eventCam_reader` and its YARP output port are unchanged.
+
+Event denoising uses **`ev::vNoiseFilter`**, from
+`event-driven/vis/filters.h`; there is no local filter implementation.
+The filter is initialized once for `WIDTH x HEIGHT`, and its temporal and
+spatial stages are enabled independently if their configured windows are
+strictly positive:
+
+| Setting | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `NOISE_FILTER` | Enable/disable the native filter |
+| `NOISE_REFRACTORY_MS` | Native temporal check window; 0 disables it |
+| `NOISE_SPATIAL_MS` | Native spatial coincidence window; 0 disables it |
+| `NOISE_RADIUS` | Native spatial neighborhood radius in pixels |
+
+The upstream native spatial filter **includes the current pixel** in its
+neighborhood search and records timestamps for rejected events. It is not
+identical to the previous project-local filter (which excluded the center).
+This matters when repeatedly firing hot pixels are present. The native
+`check()` method returns only an accept/reject boolean, so statistics report
+`noise_rejected`, not separate temporal and spatial rejection reasons.
+`raw_events = events_used + noise_rejected` counts only events with valid
+coordinates, while `invalid` is tracked separately.
+
+Filtering runs outside `surfacesMutex`. The accepted events are applied to
+EROS and SAE under the existing surface mutex **one source packet at a time**,
+not while holding the lock across the entire `readAll()` window. MoveEnet,
+optical flow, and the visualizer still take synchronized surface snapshots.
+
+**Timestamp limitation:** with `VLIB_ENABLE_TS=OFF`, the native filter and
+SAE use packet timestamps, not individual event timestamps. This affects
+fine temporal windows and optical-flow accuracy. It is not fixed by changing
+input port types.
+
+**Validation:** compare `NOISE_FILTER=false` and `true` at the same scene.
+Changing the filter implementation changes accepted-event statistics and can
+change HPE results. The new acquisition input preserves camera handling and
+all existing pose and overlay output port names.
 
 ## Simultaneous MoveEnet vs OF+KF visualization (November 2026)
 
